Project Title: Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA) Gateway: A User-Centric Security Simulator
Project Objective: This project aims to develop a simulated Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA)
gateway that balances robust backend security architecture with a seamless user experience. The
system will model how users interact with different verification methods (e.g., Email, SMS,
Authenticator App), allowing them to dynamically select their preferred method during login,
and securely update or change their MFA preferences within their profile state.
Core Features:
● Dynamic Authentication Routing: Upon successful primary login
(username/password), the system will prompt the user with their configured MFA
options, allowing them to select their preferred verification route for that specific session.
● Profile & Preference Management: Users will have a simulated dashboard where they
can add new verification methods, remove outdated ones, or switch their default primary
MFA method.
● Simulated Code Dispatch & Validation: The system will mock the dispatching of
security tokens (e.g., printing "Token 4920 sent via SMS" to the console) and validate
user input against the generated tokens with simulated expiration states.
● Fallback UX Protocol: If a user fails verification multiple times or loses access to a
method, the system will trigger a secure fallback workflow (e.g., utilizing backup
recovery codes).
Architecture: The system will rely heavily on the Strategy Design Pattern to manage the various
verification methods seamlessly.
● Interfaces: An IAuthenticator interface will define common behaviors, specifically a
generateToken() and a verifyToken() method.
● Polymorphism & Classes: Concrete classes such as EmailAuthenticator,
SMSAuthenticator, and AppAuthenticator will implement the IAuthenticator interface,
allowing the system to process any method interchangeably without altering the core
login logic.
● Encapsulation: A User class will securely encapsulate user state, containing private
attributes for credentials, a list of active IAuthenticator objects tied to their account, and a
defined default method.
● System Manager: An MFAGateway class will manage the session state, handling the
interactions between the User objects and the chosen IAuthenticator.
Interface & Implementation Details:
● Language: The system will be implemented in Java .
● User Interface: The project will utilize a Command-Line Interface (CLI) designed with
clear, user-friendly prompts to navigate the login and preference-management menus.
● Version Control: All development, including individual contributions and commit history,
will be maintained and accessible via a public GitHub repository URL.

UML Scope: The final project report will include comprehensive UML documentation mapped
to the codebase:
● Use Case Diagrams: Detailing the user's journey through logging in, selecting an
alternative MFA method, and updating profile settings.
● Sequence Diagrams: Visualizing the flow of data between the User, the MFAGateway,
and the specific Authenticator classes during a token validation event.
● Class Diagrams: Outlining the class hierarchies, interfaces, and associations that make up
the authentication strategy.